      {
        "kind": "review",
        "ok": false,
        "reason": "In src/middleware/method-not-allowed/index.ts, `listAllowedMethods` works out the mount prefix with `routePath(c)` after `next()`. By then `c.req.routeIndex` points at the last middleware that ran, not at this one. So if any path-scoped middleware runs after it, the wrong prefix gets stripped and the 405 is missed: `app.use(methodNotAllowed({app})); app.use('/api/*', mw); app.get('/api/users', h)` returns 404 for POST /api/users instead of 405 with `Allow: GET, HEAD`, and the same happens when it is installed in a mounted sub-app. Capture the route index or path before calling `next()`, or derive the prefix another way.",
        "cost_usd": 0.17759180000000002,
        "probed": true,
        "denied": []
      },
      {
        "kind": "verify",
        "ok": true,
        "reason": null,
        "cost_usd": null,
        "probed": null,
        "denied": null
      },
      {
        "kind": "review",
        "ok": false,
        "reason": "In src/middleware/method-not-allowed/index.ts, `mountPrefix` finds the mount prefix by taking the matched route pattern (e.g. `/t/:tenant/*`) and cutting the request path at that pattern's length. If the sub-app is mounted at a path with parameters or wildcards (`parent.route('/t/:tenant', sub)`, request `POST /t/acme/users`), the cut lands in the wrong place, so the method list comes back empty and the response stays 404 instead of 405 `Allow: GET, HEAD`. Strip the prefix by matching the pattern against the real request path, not by counting characters; also, when `onMethodNotAllowed` is used, headers added by inner middleware after `next()` are currently lost because `c.res` is cleared first.",
